From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 19:55:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(llama-cpp): a prototype - a model's weights leave the
 device and come back (parking)

Carried as a llama.cpp patch beside the others
(patches/0015-park-weights.patch), after the two SYCL patches before it.

For a server that holds more models than the GPU has memory for. Today a
model that has to make room is unloaded: its backend process ends, and the
next request pays a process start, the kernel programs and the first run
again - 4 to 6 s on an Intel Arc Pro B70, of which the copy of the weights
is 0.2 to 0.8 s. A PARKED model keeps its process and gives back only the
device memory of its weights; the next request copies them back.

Off unless the model's environment says so:
  GGML_SYCL_PARKABLE=1          the weight buffers are allocated so that they can park
  LLAMA_SERVER_PARK_WEIGHTS=1   with context_on_demand: when the context is freed the
                                weights are parked, the next request brings them back
  GGML_SYCL_PARK_VERIFY=1       every unparked buffer is read back and compared
Without them the backend allocates and behaves as before.

The mechanism keeps every address: on SYCL the weights are physical memory
mapped into a reserved virtual range (the extension the pool already uses),
released at a park and committed again at the same place, so no tensor,
extra or in-place reorder changes. A device with no room refuses the
request (503) and the model stays parked. Details and what was tested are
in the llama.cpp patch's own message.

STAGED, a prototype for one measurement: N park cycles on one encoder, the
park, the unpark and the first request timed apart, the same bytes after
each. The SYCL path compiles (icpx 2025.3.3) and has not run where it was
written: no device there. No new load option and no new RPC: LocalAI's side
(a parked state, two calls) is a later patch.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
---
 .../llama-cpp/patches/0015-park-weights.patch | 980 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 980 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0015-park-weights.patch

diff --git a/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0015-park-weights.patch b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0015-park-weights.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..73f1934
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0015-park-weights.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,980 @@
+park: a model's weights leave the device and come back at the same addresses
+
+A PROTOTYPE, off unless two environment switches are set. For a server that
+holds more models than the device has memory for: a model that is not
+working keeps its process, its kernel programs and its tensors, and gives
+the device memory of its weights back; the next request copies the weights
+onto the device again instead of loading the model.
+
+ggml: a buffer may implement park() and unpark() (ggml_backend_buffer_i, both
+optional). The contract is ADDRESS-STABLE: park saves the contents and frees
+the memory behind the buffer's address range, unpark commits memory at the
+same addresses and restores the contents - so every tensor, view and extra
+that refers to the buffer stays valid, and nothing that was done to the
+weights in place (ggml-sycl's reorder of quantized tensors) is undone or
+done twice. ggml_backend_buffer_can_park/park/unpark handle multi-buffers,
+all parts or none. ggml_backend_set_alloc_usage_hint() lets the caller say
+what the buffers it is about to allocate are for, which alloc_buffer cannot
+know otherwise.
+
+ggml-sycl: with GGML_SYCL_PARKABLE=1 a weight buffer is physical memory
+mapped into a reserved virtual range (sycl_ext_oneapi_virtual_mem, which the
+pool already grows with), committed in pieces of 1 GiB. park copies the
+buffer to host memory and releases the physical memory; unpark commits it
+again, copies back and frees the host copy. A device with no room refuses
+the commit and the buffer stays parked. GGML_SYCL_PARK_VERIFY=1 reads every
+unparked buffer back and compares it with what was parked (a mismatch parks
+it again and fails the unpark), and logs a checksum at each park. Both log
+their times: the copy apart from the commit or the release. Reading or
+writing a parked buffer aborts with a message. Without the switch nothing is
+allocated differently.
+
+llama: llama_model_can_park / park / unpark / is_parked over the model's
+weight buffers: every buffer that can park does, and a model with a device
+buffer that cannot is refused whole. A model may be parked only with no
+context; llama_init_from_model() refuses a parked model.
+
+server: with LLAMA_SERVER_PARK_WEIGHTS=1 and --context-on-demand, when the
+context is freed the weights are parked, and the next request unparks them
+before its context is created. A device with no room refuses that request
+as it refuses a context (503, the model stays parked, a later request tries
+again). The context canary, when on, then holds each context created on
+unparked weights to the load's output. A request refused after an unpark
+leaves the weights parked again.
+
+Tested where there is no device: tools/server/tests/unit/test_park_weights.py
+(the switch changes nothing where the weights cannot park). The cycles were
+run on CPU against a stand-in for the hook that poisons the buffer while it
+is parked: 12 requests, each on weights that had been parked, gave the
+unparked server's embeddings bit for bit; a refused unpark was answered 503
+and the next request served; weights that did not come back failed the
+canary. ggml-sycl.cpp compiles with icpx 2025.3.3; the SYCL path itself has
+not run: it needs the device.
+---
+diff --git a/ggml/include/ggml-backend.h b/ggml/include/ggml-backend.h
+index afb8633..3293616 100644
+--- a/ggml/include/ggml-backend.h
++++ b/ggml/include/ggml-backend.h
+@@ -69,6 +69,20 @@ extern "C" {
+     GGML_API ggml_backend_buffer_type_t     ggml_backend_buffer_get_type      (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
+     GGML_API void                           ggml_backend_buffer_reset         (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
+ 
++    // Parking: the device memory of a buffer released while its contents are kept on the host and its addresses stay
++    // valid; unparking puts the contents back at the same addresses. Nothing may use the buffer in between.
++    // can_park: the buffer (every part of a multi-buffer) supports it. park/unpark return false and leave the buffer
++    // as it was when they cannot; parking a parked buffer or unparking one that is not are no-ops that return true.
++    GGML_API bool                           ggml_backend_buffer_can_park      (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
++    GGML_API bool                           ggml_backend_buffer_park          (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
++    GGML_API bool                           ggml_backend_buffer_unpark        (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
++
++    // What the buffers this thread is about to allocate are for: a backend reads it in alloc_buffer, where the usage
++    // set by ggml_backend_buffer_set_usage is not known yet (weights that can park are allocated differently).
++    // GGML_BACKEND_BUFFER_USAGE_ANY, the default, says nothing. Set it back after the allocations.
++    GGML_API void                           ggml_backend_set_alloc_usage_hint (enum ggml_backend_buffer_usage usage);
++    GGML_API enum ggml_backend_buffer_usage ggml_backend_get_alloc_usage_hint (void);
++
+     // tensor copy between different backends
+     GGML_API void ggml_backend_tensor_copy  (const struct ggml_tensor * src, struct ggml_tensor * dst);
+     GGML_API void ggml_backend_tensor_copy_n(const struct ggml_tensor * const * srcs, struct ggml_tensor * const * dsts, size_t n_tensors);
+diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-backend-impl.h b/ggml/src/ggml-backend-impl.h
+index 545880b..4709a63 100644
+--- a/ggml/src/ggml-backend-impl.h
++++ b/ggml/src/ggml-backend-impl.h
+@@ -76,6 +76,14 @@ extern "C" {
+         void         (*get_tensor_n) (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer, const struct ggml_tensor * const * tensors,       void * const * data, const size_t * offsets, const size_t * sizes, size_t n_tensors);
+         // (optional) batched tensor copies: returns true if all copies were performed, false if unsupported and no copies were performed
+         bool         (*cpy_tensor_n) (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer, const struct ggml_tensor * const * srcs, struct ggml_tensor * const * dsts, size_t n_tensors);
++        // (optional) parking: release the device memory of the buffer and keep its contents and its ADDRESSES.
++        // park() saves the contents and frees the memory behind the buffer's address range; unpark() commits memory at
++        // the same addresses and restores the contents, so every tensor, view and extra that refers to the buffer stays
++        // valid across the two. Nothing may read or write the buffer in between. Both return false, leaving the buffer
++        // as it was, when they cannot (unpark: the device has no room). A backend that cannot keep addresses leaves
++        // them NULL.
++        bool         (*park)         (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
++        bool         (*unpark)       (ggml_backend_buffer_t buffer);
+     };
